From: Mihawk <163614687+mihawk-99@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] win32u: PS5: a process publishes the display only when its mode is new

Every process that took the desktop window published the PS5 driver's display (ps5_SetDesktopWindow), and publishing
is update_display_cache( TRUE ), which enumerates the Vulkan GPUs and so loads the GPU driver (libvulkan-helper.prx,
RADV) into the process. On a PS5 that is about 30 MiB of the console's system memory per process, the budget every
helper shares: a process with it held a Unix heap of 40-43 MiB and 53-61 MiB resident, one without 9 MiB and 22-28 MiB
(PS5_Proton, the PS5 Pro, 2026-10-20), and it was in a menu builder's, a console host's, an installer's and a
launcher's update dialog's, none of which draws with the GPU. That launcher's first start on the Pro was ended by the
memory guard 202 times.

The desktop window, which every process of the session sees, now carries the mode last published as a property
(width, height and rate, from the process's own WINE_PS5_REFRESH). A process publishes only when its own mode is not
that one, and then records it, so the last process with another rate still wins (patch 1187): a game with
PROTON_120HZ=1 in a 60 Hz session publishes 120 Hz as before. A process that does not publish reads the display from
the registry the first one wrote, as Wine does in every process but the one that changes it.
---
--- a/dlls/win32u/ps5drv.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/ps5drv.c
@@ -213,6 +213,14 @@
 /***********************************************************************
  *           display
  */
+/* The rate this process publishes: WINE_PS5_REFRESH, the rate the title asked of the display (120 for a game that opts
+ * in to 119.88 Hz output), else 60 */
+static UINT ps5_refresh(void)
+{
+    const char *refresh = getenv( "WINE_PS5_REFRESH" );
+    return refresh && atoi( refresh ) > 0 ? atoi( refresh ) : 60;
+}
+
 static UINT ps5_UpdateDisplayDevices( const struct gdi_device_manager *manager, void *param )
 {
     struct pci_id pci_id = {0};
@@ -228,7 +236,7 @@
     mode.dmBitsPerPel = 32;
     mode.dmPelsWidth = screen_width;
     mode.dmPelsHeight = screen_height;
-    mode.dmDisplayFrequency = refresh && atoi( refresh ) > 0 ? atoi( refresh ) : 60;
+    mode.dmDisplayFrequency = ps5_refresh();
 
     SetRect( &monitor.rc_monitor, 0, 0, screen_width, screen_height );
     monitor.rc_work = monitor.rc_monitor;
@@ -574,11 +582,26 @@
     SERVER_END_REQ;
 
     /* the prefix may hold another driver's display config: publish ours,
-     * which also gives the server the monitor that bounds the cursor */
+     * which also gives the server the monitor that bounds the cursor.
+     * Publishing enumerates the Vulkan GPUs (update_display_cache( TRUE )),
+     * which loads the GPU driver into the process: its Unix heap went from 9
+     * to 42 MiB of the console's system memory in every process that takes
+     * the desktop window, a menu builder's and a console host's too
+     * (PS5_Proton, the PS5 Pro, 2026-10-20). So a process publishes only
+     * when its mode is not the one the desktop window, which every process of
+     * the session sees, says was published last: the last one still wins. */
     if (!display_published)
     {
+        static const WCHAR published_modeW[] =
+            {'_','_','w','i','n','e','_','p','s','5','_','d','i','s','p','l','a','y','_','m','o','d','e',0};
+        const UINT64 mode = (UINT64)screen_width | (UINT64)screen_height << 16 | (UINT64)ps5_refresh() << 32;
+
         display_published = TRUE;
-        update_display_cache( TRUE );
+        if ((UINT64)(UINT_PTR)NtUserGetProp( hwnd, published_modeW ) != mode)
+        {
+            NtUserSetProp( hwnd, published_modeW, (HANDLE)(UINT_PTR)mode );
+            update_display_cache( TRUE );
+        }
     }
 }
 
